/* ============================================================
   The application's theme — the only place colour lives.

   The values come from the mock-up this screen was built from and are
   quoted as hex, because the mock-up is their source: a cream
   background, a white card, a dark green accent. A component NEVER
   writes a hex of its own — a raw colour in a component is a token
   nobody has named, and it is invisible to every later change of the
   palette.

   THE DARK THEME WAS WITHDRAWN, and that is a decision rather than an
   oversight: the switch lived in a top bar that no longer exists, and
   the mock-up has a single palette. The reason and the consequences:
   `spec/design/ui/system-states.md` § One palette. It comes back
   together with a mock-up that describes it — and then this file gets
   a second block while the components get not one branch.

   NOTE: `@theme`, not `@theme inline`. `inline` puts the DECLARED
   VALUE straight into the class, so `.text-ink` would set as a
   particular colour and redefining the variable below would stop doing
   anything — silently.
   ============================================================ */

@theme {
  /* ---- Typeface -----------------------------------------------
     Two families and their fallback stacks. The stack is part of the
     token rather than an ornament: a font from the network can fail to
     arrive, and the screen should then look plainer, not disappear. */
  --font-sans: 'Instrument Sans', system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
  --font-serif: 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;

  /* ---- Text ---------------------------------------------------- */
  --color-ink: #1b1a17;
  --color-muted: #5c584f;
  /* Darkened from `#9b978c` on 2026-09-16. The old value measured 2.92:1 on a
     white card and 2.54:1 on `--color-surface-medium` -- below AA's 4.5:1 and
     below even the 3:1 large-text floor, so the problem did not go away by
     making the text bigger. It painted the card's own Edit and Delete actions,
     every timestamp, the character counter, the footer, and -- through
     `::placeholder` -- the ONLY label the composer's fields have.
     `--color-muted` measured 7.09:1 throughout, which is what says one token
     was wrong rather than the palette.
     This value is the lightest member of the token's own hue family
     (R = B+15, G = B+11, the relationship `#9b978c` already had) that clears
     4.5:1 against the DARKEST surface this palette paints, `#f1efe8`: 4.69:1
     there, 4.95:1 on the page, 5.40:1 on a card. Lightest on purpose -- the
     token exists to be quieter than `--color-muted`, and every step darker
     spends that distinction. Held by `tests/fitness/test_design_tokens.py` and,
     where it lands on screen, by `e2e/ui/test_smoke.py`. */
  --color-faint: #6e6a5f;
  /* Text of an inactive control, and nothing else. It measures 1.82:1 and stays
     there: WCAG 1.4.3 exempts a disabled control outright. The decision is
     written down in `spec/design/ui/system-states.md` § Tokens and carried as
     the single entry of `PERMITTED_BELOW_FLOOR`, because an exemption nobody
     recorded cannot be told from an oversight. */
  --color-disabled: #c4c0b5;
  --color-inverse: #f6f5f1;
  --color-danger: #a3382c;

  /* ---- Accent. One colour for one decision on a surface. -------
     The primary button, an active link, the focus ring. Nothing else —
     green used as decoration stops meaning "this is the thing to do". */
  --color-accent: #14503a;
  --color-accent-strong: #0c3325;

  /* ---- Surfaces ------------------------------------------------
     THE NAME IS `card`, NOT `base`, and this is a trap worth recording:
     Tailwind generates a `text-X` class from every `--color-X`, so
     `--color-base` produced `.text-base { color: … }` BESIDE the
     built-in `.text-base { font-size: 1rem }`. The colour won, so every
     `text-base` on a white card painted white text on a white
     background — the body of an entry and the title of `ErrorState`
     were INVISIBLE and nothing shouted. A token's name
     may not collide with a class name from the Tailwind scale. */
  --color-card: #ffffff;
  --color-surface: #f6f5f1;
  --color-surface-medium: #f1efe8;

  /* ---- Lines. This system's primary separator, not a shadow. ---- */
  --color-hairline: #e2dfd6;
  --color-line: #dbd7cc;

  /* ---- Radii --------------------------------------------------
     Four, because the mock-up has four, and not because they sound
     nice. The name says WHAT is rounded rather than by how many pixels
     — a component that asks for "the card radius" survives a change of
     value; one that writes `rounded-[14px]` is one of six places to
     find and one to forget. Article XIII forbids the second, and for a
     while this repository broke that ban 33 times, because there was
     neither a token nor a detector.

     `card` and `control` differ by four pixels and that is the whole
     hierarchy: a surface something rests on is gentler than a thing you
     click. `control-tight` is a control INSIDE a card (the entry's edit
     fields), where the same radius as the outside would read as a
     second card. */
  --radius-modal: 18px;
  --radius-card: 14px;
  --radius-control: 10px;
  --radius-control-tight: 9px;
  --radius-chip: 7px;

  /* ---- Type sizes outside the Tailwind scale -------------------
     Two, both from the mock-up, both between `text-sm` (14px) and
     `text-base` (16px) — that is, exactly where the scale has no name,
     which is why they previously stood in components as `text-[13px]`
     (7 times) and `text-[15px]` (4 times).

     THE NAMES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT NUMBERS AND DO NOT COLLIDE WITH THE
     SCALE. This file records above the failure in which `--color-base`
     produced a `.text-base` beside the built-in one and painted white
     text on a white background. `--text-meta` and `--text-body` have no
     built-in counterpart, so that trap does not recur here. */
  --text-meta: 13px;
  --text-body: 15px;

  /* ---- The width of the content column -------------------------
     One column up to 860 px — this is not a "mobile version", which
     remains a named non-goal (`spec/invariants.md`). */
  --container-reading: 860px;

  /* ---- The only entry animation. No bounce, no scaling. --------- */
  --animate-rise: rise 0.28s ease both;

  @keyframes rise {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(6px);
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
}

